==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|mōrn}}, {{m|enm|morwen}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|morġen}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*murganaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*murginaz}} (compare West Frisian {{m|fy|moarn}}, Low German {{m|nds|Morgen}}, Dutch {{m|nl|morgen}}, German {{m|nl|Morgen}}, Danish {{m|da|morgen}}, Norwegian {{m|no|morgon}}), from pre-Germanic {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥kéno}}, {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥kóno}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥Hko}} (compare Welsh {{m|cy|bore}} ‘morning’, Lithuanian {{m|lt|mérkti}} ‘to blink, twinkle’, Skt {{term||márīcih}} ‘ray of light’), from {{m|ine-pro|*mer-}} ‘to shimmer, glisten’ (compare Greek {{m|el|μέρα|sc=polytonic}} ‘morning’). See also {{l|en|morrow}}, {{l|en|morning}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/mɔːn/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/mɔɹn/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔː(ɹ)n|lang=en}}
* {{homophone|mourn|mourne|morne|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|poetic}} [[morning|Morning]].
#* [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'', lines 165-168,
#*: But look, the '''morn''', in russet mantle clad, / Walks o'er the dew of yon high eastward hill. / Break we our watch up, and by my advice, / Let us impart what we have seen tonight

